      "check_method": "CHECK = RALPH:PATROL self-review + GROK-ANALYST (Micro tier). GROK-ANALYST returned three real defects, all accepted and FIXED rather than recorded: (sev 6) 'safe by construction' was over-claimed — the first allowlist matched the model-id pattern GLOBALLY, exempting any claude-shaped secret anywhere in the repo forever; (sev 5) no path or rule scoping, when ANDing them was available and strictly tighter; (sev 4) the synthetic negative control was a null test that could never have exercised the allowlist, so 'allowlist exonerated' was misattributed. REMEDIATION: the exemption is now three constraints ANDed — targetRules=[generic-api-key] (every dedicated credential rule still fires), paths=the two gateway files, regexes=model-id shape. AND-enforcement PROVEN empirically, not assumed: breaking the path clause alone returns both findings (2 leaks), the same config with condition=OR suppresses them, and a typo'd targetRules id is a FATAL config error rather than a silently broader exemption. NEGATIVE CONTROLS, all passing: model-id shape in a NON-gateway file FIRES (path clause binds); gcp-api-key inside the exempted path FIRES (rule clause binds); non-model secret inside the exempted path FIRES (regex clause binds); only the inspected finding is suppressed. A first NC attempt used AWS's canonical EXAMPLE keys, which gitleaks stopwords by design — caught and redone with detectable credentials. GROK's 'not verified' gap CONFIRMED REAL: a parent-root working-tree scan does NOT honour the nested submodule config and the FP reappeared. [extend] path was tried to keep one definition and FAILS two ways (fatal with useDefault; silently drops [[allowlists]] without it) — both observed, so the parent carries a documented mirror. A third FP was discovered in the process (an archived session-state assertion whose captured 'secret' is the dotted JSON key path exceptions.2.advisory_id, NOT the advisory number — read from the scanner after a first regex based on assuming it was the number suppressed nothing). GROK sev-3 (sweep rule asymmetry: bare non-zombie lockdirs never swept) INVESTIGATED and dispositioned as correct-by-design: ~/.claude/lib/lock.sh:58 reclaims a stale bare lock by atomic `mv lockdir zombie.$$`, so bare locks are owned by that reclaim path and sweeping them externally would race the mv. The sweep targets post-reclaim debris, which is exactly the .zombie.* names. FINAL STATE: parent git-mode 290 commits -> no leaks, exit 0; submodule git-mode 484 commits -> no leaks, exit 0; working-tree findings reduced to the 3 real keys in the gitignored .env. Guards 4/4 PASS, tsc 0, both repos 0 ahead / 0 behind. One self-inflicted issue raised as CF-S107-KEY-EXPOSURE.",
